  SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

  Info.plist template for the aterm.app macOS bundle.

  The release builder stamps CFBundleShortVersionString from the release version (the
  workspace [workspace.package] version, MAJOR.MINOR.0) and CFBundleVersion
  from its monotonic ledger build number; both are checked against the binary
  and manifest. The values below are only committed template
  sentinels, never shipped. The official release uses
  com.aterm.aterm; lower-level development/test bundle callers may supply a
  different identifier, which is also what their signing profile must bind.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>aterm</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>aterm</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>com.aterm.aterm</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>aterm</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<!-- Baselines only. The release builder overwrites both at bundle time
	     (crates/aterm-release/src/bundle.rs): CFBundleVersion <- the monotonic
	     RELEASES.ledger claim, CFBundleShortVersionString <- the release
	     version, canonical MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH. CFBundleVersion is a BUILD
	     NUMBER, so its baseline is the integer sentinel 0 — never a dotted
	     version. -->
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>0</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>0.0.0</string>
	<key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
	<string>11.0</string>
	<key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- aterm ships ONE universal binary (arm64 + x86_64), so on Apple silicon
	     the native slice always exists and translation is never the right
	     answer. Without this key LaunchServices is free to pick the Intel slice,
	     and it does: whenever the launching process is itself translated the
	     child inherits that architecture, and a "Open using Rosetta" checkbox
	     ticked once is remembered for the bundle forever. The Mac then shows
	     "Using Intel-based apps on a Mac with Apple silicon ... Rosetta",
	     running aterm emulated on the very hardware it was compiled for, or
	     refusing to open at all where Rosetta is not installed. Reported from
	     the field against v0.75.0, whose bundle carried neither this key nor
	     LSArchitecturePriority. The BINARY was never at fault there: both
	     slices are signed, arm64 carries platform macOS / minos 11.0, and
	     v0.74.0 has byte-identical fat structure; the defect is the missing
	     launch policy. `true` also greys out the Get Info checkbox, so the
	     state cannot be re-entered by hand. On an Intel Mac "native" is
	     x86_64, which this same binary carries, so the key costs those
	     machines nothing. -->
	<key>LSRequiresNativeExecution</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- A terminal never needs the discrete GPU, and crates/aterm-gpu renders on
	     the integrated one of a dual-GPU Mac (a development build's
	     ATERM_GPU_POWER=high seam asks for the other). This key is set so the mux may stay there. Apple's reference
	     page for the key says only "A Boolean value indicating whether an
	     OpenGL app may utilize the integrated GPU". That a Metal app without it
	     keeps the discrete chip powered for its whole life, whichever MTLDevice
	     it picks, is an expectation: Apple's current reference does not state
	     it, and no aterm build was measured with or without the key. -->
	<key>NSSupportsAutomaticGraphicsSwitching</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- A DECLARED CAPABILITY of this build's code, read by the build before it
	     at an in-session update, only after this bundle's signature and sealed
	     identity are verified (aterm-update's handoff pre-verify): this build's
	     successor side reads a CHUNKED rendezvous grant (ATRZ2G, crates/aterm-gui
	     handoff_rendezvous.rs), so the outgoing build may hand it more sessions
	     than one descriptor message carries (62), up to the protocol's 256.
	     Without the key a parent hands over at most 62 on the launched lane, as
	     every build before this one did. The value is the capability token the
	     parent also publishes in ATERM_HANDOFF_GRANT_CAPS. -->
	<key>ATermHandoffGrantChunks</key>
	<string>chunks1</string>
	<key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
	<string>public.app-category.developer-tools</string>
	<key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
	<string>Copyright 2026 Andrew Yates. Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.</string>
	<!-- TCC usage strings (macOS privacy alerts). These change the dialog TEXT
	     ONLY: macOS keys file-system consent to the RESPONSIBLE process, and
	     every program started inside an aterm session inherits aterm as that
	     process, so the alert names aterm no matter who actually opened the
	     file. A usage string is the one place aterm can say so. It does not
	     grant anything, it does not stop the alert appearing, and it must
	     never be described as the fix — see
	     docs/DESIGN-macos-tcc-prompts-2026-08-30.md §3.2. Keep each one to a
	     single sentence: macOS truncates long strings and shows them under
	     Apple's own headline. -->
	<key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Programs you run inside aterm — your shell, builds and agents — share this access to your Documents folder.</string>
	<key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Programs you run inside aterm — your shell, builds and agents — share this access to your Desktop.</string>
	<key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Programs you run inside aterm — your shell, builds and agents — share this access to your Downloads folder.</string>
	<key>NSNetworkVolumesUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Programs you run inside aterm — your shell, builds and agents — share this access to network volumes you mount.</string>
	<key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Programs you run inside aterm — your shell, builds and agents — share this access to removable disks you plug in.</string>
	<key>NSFileProviderDomainUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Programs you run inside aterm — your shell, builds and agents — share this access to files other apps sync for you, such as iCloud Drive.</string>
	<key>NSAppDataUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Programs you run inside aterm — your shell, builds and agents — share this access to other apps' data.</string>
	<key>NSAppleMusicUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Programs you run inside aterm — your shell, builds and agents — share this access to your music library.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Programs you run inside aterm — your shell, builds and agents — share this access to your photo library.</string>
	<key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>aterm configures this Mac by using the Sign Out controls in Messages and FaceTime and verifies that the accounts are signed out.</string>
	<!-- Local Network privacy (TN3179, macOS 15+): aterm is the responsible code for
	     the tools its sessions start, so without these keys `aterm machines` (and any
	     ssh typed in a tab) reaches no Mac on the LAN: an on-link connect fails with
	     "No route to host" (docs/DESIGN-machines-2026-10-16.md §1.10, §9). aterm's own
	     connections into the LAN are the fleet's (`aterm fleet join`, the survey's
	     reads of its peers); every other is a program run inside it (`aterm machines`'
	     ssh and dns-sd, ssh to a LAN host, an agent's dev server). NSBonjourServices
	     names the three service types `aterm machines scan` browses with /usr/bin/dns-sd,
	     and `_alab-vault._tcp`, the service `aterm secrets serve` registers when advertising
	     beside its `<name>.local` host (docs/DESIGN-secrets-2026-10-20.md §9). -->
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>aterm syncs approved ALab Commons devices and connects its fleet on your local network. aterm machines and programs you run inside aterm — your shell, builds and agents — share this access.</string>
	<key>NSBonjourServices</key>
	<array>
		<string>_ssh._tcp</string>
		<string>_sftp-ssh._tcp</string>
		<string>_rfb._tcp</string>
		<string>_alab-vault._tcp</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
